---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Lone Wolf">

<section class="hero">

  <div class="hero-content">

    <h1>The Lone Wolf Board Game</h1>

    <p>
      Understanding wolves through play.
    </p>

  </div>

</section>

<main>

<section class="intro">

  <h2>About the Game</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Life in Boreal Forests: The Lone Wolf</strong> is a
    science-based educational board game developed to communicate
    wildlife ecology, biodiversity, and conservation through play.
    Based on current ecological research, the game encourages
    children and families to explore the relationships between species,
    understand the ecological importance of large carnivores, and learn
    about the challenges of conserving wildlife in human-dominated
    landscapes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Designed by Dominika Bujnáková and Ekaterina Karabanina, the game combines
    scientific accuracy with engaging gameplay, allowing players to
    experience the complexity of boreal ecosystems while developing an
    appreciation for biodiversity and coexistence with wildlife.
  </p>

</section>



<section class="feature">

  <img
    src="/images/lone-wolf-board.webp"
    alt="The Lone Wolf board game"
  />

  <div>

    <h2>Why Wolves?</h2>

    <p>
      As apex predators, wolves influence prey populations, shape food
      webs, and contribute to biodiversity. Yet they are among the most misunderstood animals in Europe.
      Although they are essential for maintaining healthy ecosystems,
      they often evoke mixed feelings because of conflicts associated
      with livestock, hunting, and human activities.
    </p>

    <p>
      
    </p>

  </div>

</section>



<section class="feature reverse">

  <div>

    <h2>Learning Through Play</h2>

    <p>
      Every component of the game is grounded in ecological knowledge.
      Players learn about predator–prey relationships, food webs,
      biodiversity, and ecosystem functioning while making decisions
      that influence both wildlife and people.
    </p>

    <p>
      Rather than presenting conservation as simply right or wrong, 
      <em> The Lone Wolf board game</em> encourages discussion, critical thinking, and
      empathy towards different perspectives surrounding wildlife, especially wolves.
    </p>

  </div>

  <img
    src="/images/lone-wolf-children.webp"
    alt="Children playing the game"
  />

</section>



<section class="research">

  <h2>Research Project</h2>

  <p>
    The board game was developed not only as an educational resource,
    but also as a science communication project investigating how games
    can improve environmental education.
  </p>

  <p>
    During 2024–2025 the game was introduced into Finnish primary
    schools, where more than 500 pupils from 15 schools participated in
    workshops and classroom activities. Through questionnaires,
    observations, and discussions, the project evaluates what factors
    influence children opinions and how gameplay contributes to
    knowledge acquisition and understanding of biodiversity, wolves, and
    wildlife conservation.
  </p>

</section>



<section class="timeline">

<h2>Project Timeline</h2>

<div class="timeline-grid">

<div class="event">
<h3>2021-2022</h3>
<p>Game concept and development.</p>
</div>

<div class="event">
<h3>2022</h3>
<p>Artwork, production and pilot testing.</p>
</div>

<div class="event">
<h3>2024–2025</h3>
<p>Educational study in Finnish schools.</p>
</div>

<div class="event">
<h3>2026-Future</h3>
<p>Continued outreach, workshops and scientific publications.</p>
</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="download">

  <h2>Print Your Own Game</h2>

  <p>
    The game is available as a free print-and-play resource for
    educational and non-commercial use. Download the files and create
    your own copy for classrooms, outreach events, or family learning.
  </p>

  <a
    href="https://wildlifegenomics.wordpress.com/print-your-own-game/"
    class="download-button"
  >
    Download Print & Play
  </a>

</section>


<section class="impact">

  <h2>Conservation Impact</h2>

  <p>
    A limited number of handcrafted editions of <em>Lone Wolf</em> were
    produced. Eighteen copies were donated to the

    <a href="https://luonnonperintosaatio.fi/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
  
    <strong> Luonnonperintösäätiö (Finnish Natural Heritage Foundation)</strong></a>,
    where they were sold to support forest conservation.
  </p>

  <p>
    Together, the games raised more than
    <strong> €1,200</strong>, with proceeds directed towards the
    purchase and permanent protection of old-growth forests in Finland.
  </p>

</section>

